  1	// Command grammar-refresh vendors a tree-sitter grammar into
  2	// internal/sem/grammars/<lang> from its upstream git repository and records
  3	// where it came from in internal/sem/grammars/grammars.json. It is a
  4	// maintainer tool: it needs the network, and its output is committed, so
  5	// building entire-graph never downloads anything.
  6	//
  7	// Usage:
  8	//
  9	//	go run ./cmd/grammar-refresh -lang zig -ref v1.1.2
 10	//	go run ./cmd/grammar-refresh -lang fsharp -ref <commit> -path fsharp -allow-local-changes
 11	//	go run ./cmd/grammar-refresh -lang newlang -ref v1.0.0 -url https://github.com/o/tree-sitter-newlang
 12	//	go run ./cmd/grammar-refresh -lang rust -ref v0.24.2 -url https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-rust -generate
 13	//	go run ./cmd/grammar-refresh -lang csharp -ref v0.24.0 -tree-sitter-cli 0.26.7
 14	//
 15	// The vendored src/parser.c must be within the ABI range the go-tree-sitter
 16	// runtime loads (minABI-maxABI). A manifest entry's local_build block, or
 17	// -generate, builds it instead of copying it: the entry's patch is applied to
 18	// the checkout, then `npx tree-sitter-cli@<version> generate <args>` runs
 19	// (default --abi maxABI), which needs Node.js. The recorded revision
 20	// regenerates with the recorded version; a new revision uses @latest and
 21	// records the version it ran. -tree-sitter-cli pins one, which is the fix if
 22	// a future tree-sitter release stops generating maxABI. binding.go is never
 23	// touched, so
 24	// a new language still needs one. A grammar that changes parse results needs
 25	// an IdentityRevision bump (internal/sem/provider.go) in the same commit.
 26	//
 27	// A manifest entry with local_changes carries edits that upstream does not
 28	// have, and copying upstream verbatim would discard them. The tool refuses
 29	// such a grammar unless -allow-local-changes is passed, and the maintainer then
 30	// reapplies the steps in the entry's note. Notes survive every refresh.
